What is APR?APR stands for Annual Percentage Rate. It's a measure used to compare the yearly cost of borrowing money or earning interest on an investment across different products like credit cards, loans, etc. The APR includes not only the interest rate but also any additional fees or costs associated with the transaction.

For example, if you're looking at two credit card offers: one has a 15% interest rate and no annual fee, while another has a 10% interest rate but charges an $85 annual fee, the APR for the first card would be 15%, while the second card's APR might be higher than 15% due to the additional annual fee. 

It's important to note that the APR does not include any compound interest or other fees that may apply after the initial period of borrowing. Always check the terms and conditions before making a decision.yes
